[{:seon.task/name "source-at-the-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: in an agent, `clojure.repl/source` and `source-fn` answer a var's recorded text (interned into clojure.repl from my.agent at the agent's start, as clojure.main's REPL refers them), reading the program facts (:form/src, `seon.doc/source-of`) since program code lives as facts, not files. Reproduction it answers: agents evaluated (clojure.repl/source f) or (clojure.repl/source-fn 'ns/f) 171 times in one run (\"No such var: clojure.repl/source\"), and slurped a resource path 53 times (\"Cannot open <nil> as a Reader\"). Owed: its proof, for a program (not platform) definition. Estimate: one AVET seek per call (`seon.db/eid`'s cost)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/source-answers-a-program-definition-from-its-facts}}
 {:seon.task/name "eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an eval record's :form is the form printed under the :form budget (600 tokens), so a failing form is cut (…N), and a defn that failed could only be recovered from the model's reply. Correct: for a form that errs, record the exact text the agent sent (cut from the reader's positions, as :var/src is for defs), bounded only by the request itself; a form that succeeds keeps its printed echo. Cost follows the failing forms only."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-failing-form-is-recorded-whole}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-error-never-ends-the-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: run seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took#1 ended :closed :error at turn 0, reason \"seon.turn/work! threw: No matching field found: exists for class java.lang.String\", error {:seon.error/type :seon.agent/eval-error :seon.error/operation seon.repl/eval-code :class IllegalArgumentException}; its branch shows every form of `my.root` re-evaluated unchanged (a reload or load-file). A plain (load-file \"src/my/root.clj\") or (require 'my.root :reload) in a fresh agent answers nil (not reproduced). Cause: an evaluation error escaped `seon.repl/eval-code` into `seon.turn/work!`, and the turn was not recorded, so the form that raised it is lost. Correct: find the form and path that raise it (reload my.root after the agent's other turn-0 forms; a load whose form calls a File method on a path string); every error raised while a loaded file's forms evaluate reaches the agent as that load's :err item (as Clojure's require throws and the REPL prints it), never the end of its run; record the turn before anything after the evaluation can fail."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-error-inside-a-loaded-file-is-the-loads-error-item}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-item-shape-for-an-eval"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.repl/record-tx` stores a reply item's :printed as :shown and :err as :error, so ``seon.render.ui/eval-out`` reads (or :printed :shown). Storing the item's own keys saves about 3 lines, but stored records would still need :shown read beside :printed: a permanent dual reader, the fallback AGENTS §9 names. Not worth doing as a rename alone; worth doing only if the stored :seon.eval/record values are rewritten too (the standing store is test data, erased: DECISIONS: The standing store is test data), so one reader remains."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "type-findings-are-an-evals-warnings"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt checks the success types of calls (`jolt.passes.types/check-form`, a public function returning [{:op :argpos :type :msg :pos}] for provably wrong calls, non-strict by default), but only prints them to stderr when the environment variable JOLT_CHECK is set (host/chez/compile-eval.ss); stderr reaches an agent only after its death, so an eval never shows them. Our REPL already holds each analyzed node: `seon.repl/wrap-record` binds `jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*` to `seon.program/tap`, which is called with [form node], and a reply item has a `:warnings` key (`seon.repl/wrap-instrument` fills it with `:uncontracted-definition`). Correct: a small step of the same wrap calls `check-form` on each node the tap holds and adds its findings to `:warnings` as data (no environment variable, nothing refused: it records, AGENTS §4), rendered with the item. Measured silent on all of `src` (3,397 forms, 0 findings: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics); not measured yet: the cost per form, owed as a cost proof of work done. Gate: the owner's go, since it adds content to an eval item (AGENTS §3 root's gate, 4). Deletes nothing; it replaces the idea of setting JOLT_CHECK. Jolt's JOLT_DIAG (the CLI's uncaught-error report), JOLT_DEBUG (build and cache diagnostics) and its nREPL server are deliberately unused: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-provably-wrong-call-is-a-warning-on-its-item seon.repl-test/a-checked-form-costs-its-nodes seon.repl-test/our-own-source-loads-with-no-type-warnings}}
 {:seon.task/name "err-text-is-the-err-stream"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an eval item's :err-text is `(str err (nrepl/err->msg v {:outer outer}))` (`seon.repl/eval-code`, two more producers at the read error and the entrant boundary), so it holds two things under one key: what the form wrote to `*err*`, and a second error render (Jolt's text, its position request-relative). The error's own render is :printed (`seon.render.ai/error`) and the error map's data carries the position in the form's terms (:seon.error/at). Only `repl_test` reads :err-text (`a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text` asserts it includes the divide-by-zero message). Correct: the `*err*` stream is its own item key beside :out (`:stderr`, present when the form wrote anything), the err->msg text and the `outer-frames` capture that feeds only it are deleted, and the test asserts the error's :printed. Deletes: :err-text from the item keys (`seon.repl` item-keys), the three producers, `outer-frames`. Replaces a second render by the one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-response-carries-values-not-their-printed-text seon.repl-test/a-form-that-writes-to-err-answers-it-as-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it (AGENTS.md section 9: a clipping function). Left in src: seon.root records a dead agent's stderr as its last tail-bytes (2048) though the file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`) is kept beside the store. Gone already: `seon.agent/error-map`'s message-chars cut, seon.model's 300-char HTTP error body, seon.learn's 80-char form and seon.agent-proc's 200-char form in :last-op and :running. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the stderr file's path and size); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes tail-bytes (max-assertions is gone: every failing assertion is kept)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole
                      seon.problems-test/a-red-assertion-is-persisted
                      seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-record-stores-its-value-within-a-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.repl/record-details` stores any plain value whole, on main too for root's outside and UI evals, with an O(value) plain? walk and no bound: storage dedupes by content key, but main grows with every large value. Correct: state the bound (a config key, the value's size by the store's own measure) past which a value is stored as its printed form and type, as a non-plain one is, saying so; or measure that none is needed. Proof: a record past the bound holds :printed and :type and says why; one under it holds the value."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-record-has-its-declared-shape"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on batch 89's cause (Datahike's \"Query for unknown vars\" records reached :seon.eval/record): \"How do we have values that can't be saved if we have schemas that map to the datahike db?\", then \"The fix your question points at is to declare the response's real shape: correct\". What exists: :seon.eval/record (resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn) declares [:response :map], open, so a value no store keeps passed the contract and only seon.db/plain? caught it at the write; batch 89 made it storable at the build (seon.repl/plainly in seon.repl/record-details) and answered a still refused record :unrecorded (seon.repl/record!). The change: declare :response's keys and types from what seon.repl/eval-string answers (:handle, :value, :ns, :ms, :ticks, :bytes, :forms, :again, :out, :err, :ex, :repaired and the rest, each from its producer), and the error map its own schema (:seon.error/base's keys and the eval error's :jolt.error/* diagnostics, :class, :causes) with its :data restricted to plain data (one schema for plain data, seon.db/plain? as a Malli predicate, :seon.db/plain); record-details is instrumented, so a value no store keeps is invalid there, explained, naming the field and the function. Then plainly and :unrecorded (by the owner's rule of the day, AGENTS section 3 step 4: remove what allowed the class): the conversion of an exception's data (a library's: its records kept as their text) moves out of record-details to the one place a host Throwable becomes Seon's error map, and if more than one place builds error maps from Throwables (seon.agent/error-map, seon.error's constructors, a turn's, a run's or an end's error), they become one, so every stored error is plain by construction and the error schema's :data is :seon.db/plain with no guard downstream; :unrecorded stays for a write the store refused that no contract sees (the store full or failing), a resource failure, another class. The same open typing elsewhere, judged: :seon.turn/error [:map] (seon.facts.edn), :seon.run/error and :seon.end/error [:map] and :seon.error/example [:map] (seon.problems.edn), all stored and all an error: the same gap, each declared as the error schema; :seon.merge/key, base, ours and theirs [:any] (seon.facts.edn) are stored program values (a datom's value, a form's text): legitimately any, but any storable one, so :seon.db/plain, not :any; :seon.schema/definition :any (seon.schema.edn) is a stored Malli form, plain data by Malli's own form: :seon.db/plain; :seon.db/tx-data [:sequential :any] is a transaction, never stored itself (its datoms are, each by its attribute): no gap; :seon.print/hiccup and :seon.print/node are the printer's values, not stored: no gap. :seon.ctx/pattern [:vector :any] IS stored (an attribute, resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn, held by :seon.db/agent-fact, the agent's own edit of its pull pattern that seon.ctx's pattern-of reads): a Datahike pull pattern, declared as Datahike's pull grammar if Malli expresses it cheaply, else :seon.db/plain. The bug-class audit (2026-11-03) lists the Throwable-to-error sites the one constructor absorbs, with three ex-data policies (raw, bounded, encoded text) collapsing into one: seon.agent/error-map, seon.repl/located, seon.root/flow-error, seon.render/render!'s component defect (raw ex-data under :seon.render/threw: keeps its raw ex-data only while it never reaches a store or the wire, so it is checked), seon.render.proc/pass-view's view defect, seon.server/respond, seon.proofs, seon.print.select/throwable-value, my.agent's pst and seon.lib's forked-child print; and :seon.error/base becomes [:and its keys [:map-of :keyword :seon.db/plain]], so every error is plain by its constructor. Proof: an eval whose response holds a non-plain value is invalid at record-details, naming the field; the stored error attributes refuse a non-plain :data at their contract."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/the-store-keeps-one-plain"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-record-with-a-value-no-store-keeps-is-invalid-where-it-is-built}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-repl-fallback-keeps-its-forms-position"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by batch 87's review (the REPL's names in every namespace, seon.agent/repl-name over Jolt's jolt.host/*resolve-unresolved*). Three follow-ups in Jolt, on a jolt/<topic> branch of seantempesta/jolt: (1) jolt.analyzer/analyze-list*'s fallback arm analyzes (cons fallback (rest items)) without the form's metadata, so an error inside a call through the fallback loses its line and column; carry (meta form) onto the rewritten form. (2) fallback-head calls resolve-global on every call head nothing shadows while the hook is bound, a second resolve of the head; reuse the resolution the macro arm makes, or measure it (jolt's analyzealloc gate, an agent's eval at n <= 100) and record that it is noise. (3) the unit corpus covers the hook under eval but not jolt.loader's unbinding: a require of a file holding an unresolved name still errors with the hook bound. The gates run as for any pin (docs/merging.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-file-loaded-at-the-repl-resolves-as-written}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-values-do-not-echo-a-whole-state"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, forms: (1) `(in-ns 'my.task)` in one eval leaves the next eval in my.task, where `source` and other helpers the agent used before are unresolved (\"Unable to resolve symbol: source in this context\"): say the current namespace on each answer, or resolve the REPL helpers from any namespace. (2) (swap! seon.agent/state update :turn dissoc :observed) answered the whole state map, about 25k tokens in :values (its :results held every earlier eval's value): a value of that size should be cut by the printer with the form that reads the rest, as (:out result/eN) is. Proof: after in-ns the answer names the namespace; an atom's state answered under a bound prints cut with its read form."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-eval-answer-names-its-namespace-and-cuts-a-whole-state}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-runs-in-one-mode"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S3-S6; checked against main 2026-10-27) and the blocking-calls audit (row 2). What exists: seon.repl/run! picks jolt.host/main-carrier-run! only when (lib/macos?) (repl.clj:573), seon.intr registers signal wakes only when (jolt.host/main-carrier?) (intr.clj:80, 92), and seon.sock/recv! chooses a parking read or the blocking recvmsg arm by (and main-carrier? fiber?) (sock.clj:195). The agent therefore forks safely and parks on macOS and neither elsewhere: each branch defends the other mode. Correct: the agent runs on the main carrier on every OS (needs jolt.io-poller/the-io-poller-wakes-on-signals-on-linux), recv! is always MSG_DONTWAIT plus poller/wait-ready, and seon.lib/macos? (lib.clj:219), the park? test and the blocking arm are deleted. Proof to write: an agent on Linux parks on root's socket and answers an interrupt."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-docstring-redefinition-is-a-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (root's end-to-end reuse measurement, 2026-10-27, on .worktrees/reuse-end-to-end's root at main 6b34178937): an outside agent evaluated `(in-ns 'seon.render.ui) (def utc-offset-ms \"Measured: Jolt has no time zones ...\" (let ...)) (in-ns 'user)`, the shipped form with only its docstring changed. The eval answered no :recorded (a defn changed in its body answers :recorded [seon.merge/divergence]), `(my.branch/changes)` did not list it, and the program digest did not move, so no test's digest moved and the service answered from its memo. Correct: a docstring is part of what a var is (help, the wiki's citations, the page), so a redefinition that changes only it is recorded as a change of the var, and a test whose reach includes the var takes the new form's digest. Not yet known: whether this is a def-only gap (a defn's docstring) or a docstring-only gap; the first proof decides. Owner: seon.repl (the eval's record), with seon.program's account if the text is cut before hashing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-docstring-only-redefinition-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "print-length-is-the-sessions"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 (the optimization session, at root's REPL): (set! *print-length* 5) answers \"Can't change/establish root binding of: *print-length* with set\", and the printer shows a response with *print-length* and *print-level* nil (seon.print.emit binds them off). In clojure.main/repl and nREPL each session binds *print-length*, *print-level* and *print-meta* (as it binds *1 *2 *3 *e), so set! works and the value is printed under them: the well-known way a Clojure programmer asks for a smaller answer (docs/agent.md#small-answers). Seon's REPL binds *1 *2 *3 *e from :repl-vars (seon.repl's exec!) and not these. Correct: the session's *print-length* and *print-level* are kept beside :repl-vars, bound around each eval as clojure.main does, and the response's value is shown under them by the one printer (a collection past *print-length* shows its first items and the form that reads the rest by the handle, as any cut does); unset, nothing changes (an agent's own results are never cut unless it asks). Deletes nothing; replaces no mechanism (the printer's cut is the one cut)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/set-print-length-limits-the-sessions-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-failure-is-its-ex"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: a failed call is either a thrown :ex or an error map returned as :value, recognised by contract/error? (any map with a keyword :seon.error/type); af8b9c2af5 made seon.turn stop a reply's later calls on either, and 0a58e8aa31 then had to move grep's hint out of a value. Readers that read only :ex, so an error value reads as success: seon.turn's turn log :error, seon.render.ui's event row and its err? (the cross and the red border), seon.repl's record :line, seon.reconcile; and :ex itself is a string or a map (ui tests map?, the record line ors the two). The change: in :seon.eval/response (seon.core.edn) :ex is the one failure member, typed as the error schema, never a string; seon.repl/eval-string, the one producer, answers a form whose value is an error map as :ex; every reader reads :ex alone, and seon.turn drops its contract/error? arm. A tool that advises without failing answers a value of a declared schema (grep's :my.agent/grep-none), as the rule. Docs: docs/agent.md#the-repl and docs/render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.repl/an-evals-record-has-its-declared-shape"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-error-value-is-the-evals-failure}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-commit-that-keeps-failing-ends-no-agent-silently"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen in review, 2026-11-04: `seon.repl/serve!`'s loop over the flow's error channel commits what is dirty after answering a defect. `seon.agent/commit!` clears :dirty? only after a successful commit, so a commit that keeps failing throws again inside that loop, which has no recur after it: the loop ends, no later defect is answered, and every later asker waits forever, against AGENTS section 4 (nothing crashes and nothing hangs: every wait ends with its value or a typed error). Not reproduced yet: first make the store refuse a commit in an agent, and see it. The class: the error path's own effect failing. Correct: a commit's failure at this edge is an error value (named in docs/failure-points.md with its one edge function, and the requester told), and the loop always recurs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-commit-that-keeps-failing-still-answers}}]
